```cpp
class MyString {
private:
    char* _data;
    size_t _length;
public:
    // ... constructors, destructor, copy constructor from previous card ...

    // Copy Assignment Operator (Deep Copy with self-assignment protection)
    MyString& operator=(const MyString& other) {
        if (this == &other) { // Self-assignment check
            return *this;
        }

        // 1. Deallocate old resources
        delete[] _data;

        // 2. Allocate new resources
        _length = other._length;
        _data = new char[_length + 1];

        // 3. Copy data
        strcpy(_data, other._data);

        return *this; // Return reference to *this
    }
    // ... other members ...
};
```
*Self-assignment protection* (`if (this == &other)`) prevents deleting the source data before copying it, which would lead to data loss. The *copy-and-swap idiom* is a more robust, exception-safe alternative.
